{
  "abstract": "For a putative rational Diophantine septuple A, paper 40 defines a triangle-free graph G(A): p and q are adjacent when A minus {p,q} is a regular quintuple. Papers 45, 46, 49 and 52 treat maximum degree at most two. Here we treat a vertex of degree four, whose four neighbours are the roots of the order-three quartic S(a,b,.) of Dujella, Kazalicki, Mikic and Szikszai. We prove that G(A) has at most one vertex of degree four. That leaves 23 of the 107 triangle-free graphs on seven vertices. For each one we solve the full system of root equations, cycle-lemma relations and Dujella's quintic condition for every extra edge, with all entries and differences inverted. Ten graphs carry no configuration over the algebraic closure. Six carry finitely many, all computed exactly; none is rational, and every Galois orbit has degree at least 4. These counts rest on msolve's multimodular characteristic-zero computation; every listed point is verified exactly. So a rational septuple with a degree-four vertex has one of seven graphs: three of dimension at least two and four curves. The quintic identity gives F2-relations among the square classes of the 21 values. We compute the quotient rank r for every remaining graph (7, 3, 3, 2, 2, 2, 1), checking it against Legendre symbols over finite fields and against exact square classes on the finite orbits. On the row-39 curve all 21 values share one square class, so a rational point there with one square condition is already a rational septuple. Adjoining the forced square roots to the finite orbits gives explicit Diophantine septuples whose graph has a degree-four vertex, over octic fields (including a totally real one) and fields of degree 16. They are verified exactly, independently of the solver. No rational septuple is constructed and none is excluded.",
